Context()->variables->are(T => 'Real'); $h = -460; $r = 0.2; $f = Formula("$r (T - $h)")->reduce; $gt = GraphTool("{line,solid,(40,100),(90,110)}")->with( bBox => [-10,120,120,-10], availableTools => [ "LineTool" ], gridX => 10, gridY => 10, ticksDistanceX => 20, ticksDistanceY => 20, snapSizeX => 10, snapSizeY => 2, showCoordinateHints => 0, showInStatic => 0, xAxisLabel => 'x\\text{ (℉)}', yAxisLabel => 'y\\text{ (lb/in²)}' );
###### # Body ######
BEGIN_PGML The pressure in a full propane tank will rise and fall if the ambient temperature rises and falls. The equation [`P=[$f]`] models this relationship, where the temperature [`T`] is measured in °F and the pressure and the pressure [`P`] is measured in lb/in^2. Plot a graph of this equation. Make sure to use [`T`]-values that make sense in context.
[_]{$gt}{1}
END_PGML
